==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|frm|harassement}}. {{surf|en|harass|-ment}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/həˈɹæs.mənt/|/ˈhæɹ.əs.mənt/|/-mɪnt/}}
** See [[harass]] for pronunciation note.
* {{audio|en|En-us-harassment.oga|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-harassment.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{1|persistent}} [[attack]]s and [[criticism]] causing [[worry]] and [[distress]].
# {{1|deliberate}} [[pester]]ing or [[intimidation]].
# {{lb|en|military}} The use of [[repeat]]ed [[small-scale]] [[attack]]s to [[wear down]] an [[enemy]] [[force]].
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=The Battle of Samar - Odds? What are those?|date=27 February 2019|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4AdcvDiA3lE|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221103043318/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4AdcvDiA3lE|archivedate=3 November 2022|accessdate=5 November 2022|time=27:22|text=Throughout this period, and beyond, into the rest of the battle, aircraft of various types and loadouts are crisscrossing the skies in desperate '''harassment''' attacks, with the pilots having to play constant games of "guess the carrier" to decide where to land as escort carriers are hit, sunk, disappear in columns of shell splashes, or are forced to evade at angles to the wind that make landing on them impossible.}}

